.TH BOOTTRACE 8
.SH NAME
boottrace \- trace every system call from the boot
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B node k/build/src/main/node.js --ratrace=\fIfile\fP
[
.I option ...
]
.B --
.I command
.PP
.IB url ?ratrace
.PP
.B ratrace=1
.SH DESCRIPTION
.IR Ratrace (1)
attaches to a process that already runs,
so p9web's kernel can trace the boot itself:
every system call of every process from the first, pid 1,
in
.IR ratrace 's
format,
a line for each call, written whole when the call ends,
and an
.B Exits
line when it starts.
.PP
Under Node,
.B --ratrace=\fIfile
writes the trace to the host's
.IR file .
On the page,
.B ?ratrace
in its URL, or a line
.B ratrace=1
in its
.IR plan9.ini (8),
turns it on.
Either way the kernel keeps the trace, up to 64 megabytes, in
.BR /dev/ratrace .
A read of
.B /dev/ratrace
sees the lines that were there when its reader opened the file,
so a reader under the trace does not feed itself.
.SH EXAMPLES
Draw the boot so far, in a new window:
.IP
.EX
aux/ratrace2tl /dev/ratrace | rtrace -w
.EE
.SH FILES
.B /dev/ratrace
.SH SOURCE
plan9web's
.B k/src/kernel/kernel.ts
and
.B k/src/kernel/dev.ts
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.IR ratrace (1),
.IR rtrace (1),
.IR plan9.ini (8)
.SH BUGS
The trace keeps its first 64 megabytes and drops the lines after them.
